      "chunk_type": "worked_example",
      "content_markdown": "Example 23. If $f(y) = \\frac{y^3 - 3y^2 + 1}{y(1 - y)}$, then show that $f\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right) = f(1 - y)$\n\nSolution: $f(y) = \\frac{y^3 - 3y^2 + 1}{y(1 - y)}$\n\n$$\\therefore f\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right) = \\frac{(\\frac{1}{y})^3 - 3(\\frac{1}{y})^2 + 1}{\\frac{1}{y}(1 - \\frac{1}{y})} = \\frac{\\frac{1 - 3y + y^3}{y^3}}{\\frac{y - 1}{y^2}}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{1 - 3y + y^3}{y^3} \\times \\frac{y^2}{y - 1} = \\frac{1 - 3y + y^3}{y(y - 1)}$$\n\nAgain, $f(1 - y) = \\frac{(1 - y)^3 - 3(1 - y)^2 + 1}{(1 - y)(1 - (1 - y))}$\n\n$$= \\frac{1 - 3y + 3y^2 - y^3 - 3(1 - 2y + y^2) + 1}{(1 - y)(1 - 1 + y)}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{1 - 3y + 3y^2 - y^3 - 3 + 6y - 3y^2 + 1}{y(1 - y)}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{-1 + 3y - y^3}{y(1 - y)} = \\frac{-(1 - 3y + y^3)}{-y(y - 1)}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{1 - 3y + y^3}{y(y - 1)}$$\n\n$$\\therefore f\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right) = f(1 - y)$$",
